#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199372} :activity/id #uuid "c6311230-e135-5677-8edf-6ab9b7341bf2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786 :topic/id #uuid "bea5c820-dd0e-5c3b-936d-578694b34907" :topic/math-academy-id 4171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199501 :tutorial/content "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "31dda75b-f239-5680-b7b4-0dce1ba5e51f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7846 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b4dea87-c57c-5435-9e3b-1027840f045c" :step/math-academy-id 45308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198076 :question/id #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12562"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049019}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281604}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "b898729c-517e-588b-8fc2-30ad336c09b1" :step/math-academy-id 45309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81df7b3f-d26d-54e0-baa0-de20a949b348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7847 :tutorial/title "The Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3baad2a8-726c-5a26-b651-c058c266b286" :step/math-academy-id 45312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199503 :tutorial/content "The formula derived earlier works for multivariable functions with a finite number of variables. \n\nSuppose that the function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor approximation of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & \\approx f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the first derivative is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the second derivative is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9075c096-fb26-52c7-ba1b-f03ecebd24e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7848 :tutorial/title "General Formula For the Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3af1e2e0-59c9-5db1-ae9b-2ec6c9cd91fb" :step/math-academy-id 45313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199376}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198077 :question/id #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367825}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "37ed5f21-d5e5-5cba-a75e-2083bfa15f8a" :step/math-academy-id 45314 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199504 :tutorial/content "Here, we derive the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form.\n\nSuppose that the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a}.$\nBy restricting $\\mathbf x$ to a line through $\\mathbf{a},$ we can consider $f$ as a function of one variable. Then, we'll use the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function to derive the formula.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{x}_0$ be a constant vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then, the line through $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}), \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}(1) = \\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nNow, we define a function $F: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by restricting the domain of $f$ to this line:\n\n$$\nF(t)\n= f(\\mathbf{x}(t))\n%= f(\\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}))\n$$\n\nThe second-degree Taylor polynomial of this single-variable function about $t=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(t) \\approx F(0) + F'(0)t + \\dfrac12F''(0)t^2.\n$$\n\nWe can find the first derivative of $F$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a+t({x}_{0}-a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}$ is a row vector and $(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a})$ is a column vector, this is a scalar quantity and, hence, equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a))}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith this, we can find the second derivative of $F{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}F}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}) \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first and second derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a) \\\\ {F}^{''}(t)=({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function and its derivatives at $t=0$ and using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}F(0)=f(x(0)) \\\\ {F}^{'}(0)={f}^{'}(x(0))({x}_{0}-a) \\\\ {F}^{''}(0)=({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(x(0))({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting these values into the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function about $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & \\approx F(0)+{F}^{'}(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}{F}^{''}(0){t}^{2} \\\\  & \\approx f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)({x}_{0}-a)t+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)({x}_{0}-a){t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using this polynomial to approximate the value of $F(1),$ and the fact that $F(1) = f(\\mathbf{x}(1)) = f(\\mathbf{x}_0),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & \\approx f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\ f({x}_{0}) & \\approx f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)({x}_{0}-a)+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\mathbf{x}_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is an arbitrary constant. Therefore, we can relax the assumption that $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is fixed and replace it with a general point $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ near $\\mathbf{a}$ to obtain the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de526622-dda2-5168-9ec1-6b123e04fa54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7862 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "b64ce030-6e67-5cb0-926b-59488cada120" :step/math-academy-id 45339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198078 :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367908}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "ac6cf855-2b6f-5681-9ea0-41408d0715ca" :step/math-academy-id 45340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199377}}] :activity/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198498}]}
